id: k8s.api-server
kind: kubernetes
title: API server
aliases: kube-apiserver, api server
tags: architecture, control-plane
questions:
  - what does the API server actually do?
invariant: The API server is the single front door: it authenticates, authorizes, validates and persists objects, and every other component talks only to it.
answer_30s: Every component and user goes through the API server: it authenticates, runs RBAC, validates the object against its schema, stores it in etcd, and informs watchers of the change.
answer_2m: |
  Two properties worth stating: it is stateless (all state is in etcd, so
  several replicas run behind one endpoint) and it is the single choke point
  where policy applies (RBAC, admission webhooks, validation). Nothing else in
  the system holds the source of truth. That is why kubectl failing with a TLS
  or connection error stops every automation at once, and why an overloaded API
  server looks like everything breaking at the same time -- the operators still
  work, the watch stream does not.
when_to_use:
  - understanding permissions, validation, and why all automation depends on it
commands:
  - kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n payments => RBAC decision for the current identity
  - kubectl api-resources => which kinds exist and whether they are namespaced
failure_modes:
  - API server or etcd overloaded, so watches lag and controllers stall
  - admission webhook down, blocking all writes to matched kinds
related:
  - k8s.etcd
  - k8s.controller
  - k8s.namespace
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/
